          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#explanation"} Explanation
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#on_complex_projective_space"} On Complex Projective Space
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#projective_submanifolds"} Projective Submanifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_general_case"} The General Case
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relationship_to_kostantsouriau_operators"} Relationship to Kostant-Souriau Operators
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called the “geometrization of quantum mechanics” by
            %a{:href => "#Kibble79"} Kibble (1979)
            who introduced the idea, or “geometrical formulation of quantum mechanics” &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#AshtekarSchilling99"}> Ashtekar &amp; Schilling (1999)
            \&amp;rbrack; or just “geometric quantum mechanics” &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#BrodyHughston01"}> Brody &amp; Hughston (2001)
            \&amp;rbrack;, but which ought to be called the “symplectic formulation”, is the observation that, in standard
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+projective+space"} complex projective space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \mathscr{H}
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+quantum+states"} pure quantum states
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
                is canonically a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
                (via the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fubini-Study+metric"} Fubini-Study metric
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curves"} curves
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \mathscr{H}
                which correspond to solutions of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flows"} Hamiltonian flows
                with respect to this symplectic structure.
          %p
            This is fairly immediate to see from mathematical inspection, but the perspective is somewhat surprising from the point of view of standard accounts of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flows"} Hamiltonian flows
            as the hallmark of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation"} deformation
            of this symplectic structure (whence: “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"}> deformation quantization
            ”).
          %p
            It follows that instead of thinking of a quantization of a symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\omega)
            as some kind of deformation to operators on a Hilbert space, one can think of it as merely replacing the classical mechanics of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with the classical mechanics of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathcal{H}).
          %p Now, the standard perspective is certainly not wrong, but various authors have inevitably suggested that the “geometrical formulation” (more descriptive would be: “symplectic formulation”) of quantum mechanics may point to some deeper truth, and if only to show some kind of conceptual unity where one is used to amplifying the dichotomy.
          %h2#explanation Explanation
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M^{2n},\omega)
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            (which is always of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\omega^n
            for some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}\to M
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mn 3
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta:M^3\to\mathbb{C}
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          %p
            Then there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between:
          %ol
            %li#Item1
              %p
                (
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} Path integral quantization
                ) Quantizations obtained via a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
                over
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curves"} curves
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M,
                whose amplitude is given by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
                and whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-point+function"} 3-point function
                is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta.
            %li#Item2
              %p
                (
                %strong>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} Geometric quantization
                ) The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
                given by a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
                and whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+2-form"} curvature
                is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi VE
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi log
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} VE(\log{\Delta})
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi VE
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} VE
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Est"} van Est
                map).
            %li#Item3
              %p
                (
                %strong> Berezin quantization
                ) Maps
              #eq:qMapsMToP.maruku-equation
                %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi M
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      q \colon M \to P(\mathcal{H})\subset B(\mathcal{H}),
              %p with the overcompleteness property:
              #eq:OvercompletenessProperty.maruku-equation
                %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi ℋ
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mi M
                        %mi q
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi d
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      1_{\mathcal{H}} = \textstyle{\int_M} q\,d\mu
              %p
                and whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-point+function"} 3-point function
                is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta.
                Here we are using the identification of points in projective space with rank–one orthogonal projections.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Item2"} Item 2
            presents by far the most common way of thinking about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msubsup
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ×
                  %mi M
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0^*\mathcal{L}^*\otimes \pi_1^*\mathcal{L}\to M\times M\,,
          %p
            and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifolds"} Poisson manifolds
            we can replace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ×
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\times M
            by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebra"} convolution algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mi γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega(a,b)=\int_{\gamma(0)=a}^{\gamma(1)=b}\mathcal{D}\gamma\,P(\gamma)\in\text{Hom}(\mathcal{L}_{a},\mathcal{L}_{b})\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\gamma)
            is parallel transport over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma.
          %p
            In the most common examples,
            %a{:href => "#Item2"} item 2
            is given by holomorphic sections of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Item3"} item 3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+map"} classifying map
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℒ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}
            and points in its image are called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+states"} coherent states
            – it is not just any classifying map though, it has an overcompleteness property. This is
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Berezin"} Berezin
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a{:href => "#Item1"} item 1
            is how
            %a{:href => "#klauder"} Klauder
            conceived of quantization. For a quote:
          %blockquote
            %p
              “all these different procedures to quantize phase space [can physically] be thought of as just different ways of regularizing and defining properly the path integral” &amp;lbrack;
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Davide+Gaiotto"}> Davide Gaiotto
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/EwwGAf2K1uQ?si=eG8E6ILPOtN10obr&t=220"} yt
          %p
            That something like this was true seems to have been first made explicit in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Odzijewicz92"} Odzijewicz 1992
          %h3#on_complex_projective_space On Complex Projective Space
          %p
            The simplest and most natural example of this is when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+space"} finite dimensional
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+projective+space"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M=P(\mathcal{H})
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fubini-Study+metric"} Fubini–Study
            form.
            %a{:href => "#Item1"} Item 1
            and
            %a{:href => "#Item3"} item 3
            are very natural to describe here, while
            %a{:href => "#Item2"} item 2
            is a bit more complicated but quickly follows from the description of 1. We’ll first describe item 3.
          %p
            In this example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:qMapsMToP"} (1)
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:OvercompletenessProperty"} (2)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle\rangle
                  \\,\colon\,
                  B(\mathcal{H})
                  \\to
                  C^{\infty}\big(P(\mathcal{H})\big)
                  \\,,\quad
                  \\langle H\rangle\big([v]\big)
                  \\,=\,
                  \\langle v|H|v\rangle
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            is any normalized representative of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [v]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+value"} expectation value
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            in the state
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [v]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/imaginary+unit"} imaginary unit
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{i}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{H})
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathcal{H})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{i}\langle \rangle
            being a Lie algebra morphism is that, with respect to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+observables"} classical observables
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle H\rangle\,,
            the classical and quantum mechanics are the same. That is,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi t
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi H
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathrm{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\Psi
                  \\,=\,
                  H\Psi
          %p
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\mapsto [\Psi](t)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flow"} Hamiltonian flow
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle H\rangle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in B(\mathcal{H})
            satisfies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg's
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
            with respect to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle A\rangle
            satisfies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equation"} Hamilton's equations of motion
            with respect to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle H\rangle.
          %p
            There is also a natural map from classically mixed states to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mixed+states"} quantum mixed states
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrices"} density matrices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi q
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f\mapsto Q_f
                  \\,=\,
                  \\int_{P(\mathcal{H})} f\,q\,\omega^n
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\ge 0
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int_{P(\mathcal{H})}f\,\omega^n
                  \\,=\,
                  1\,.
          %p From the overcompleteness property (#OvercompletenessProperty), it follows that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi f
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Tr(Q_{f}A)
                  \\,=\,
                  \\int_{P(\mathcal{H})} f\,\langle A\rangle \omega^n\,.
          %p
            This means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \rangle
            also preserves the state structure. Mathematically,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi Q
                      %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\rangle=Q^{\dagger}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            is the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_f
            is known as
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Berezin"} Berezin
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \rangle
            is known as Berezin’s covariant symbol. Of course,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is really defined on any reasonably nice function — in general it should be interpreted as an operator, not a state.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            agrees with the Berezin–Toeplitz quantization &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Schlichenmaier2010"}> Schlichenmaier 2010
            \&amp;rbrack;. Let us emphasize that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⇒
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi f
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\ge 0\implies Q_f\ge 0
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Tr(Q_{f})
                  \\,=\,
                  \\int_{P(\mathcal{H})} f\, \omega^n\,.
          %p
            These are important properties of a quantization map to have, since this is exactly what is needed for it to preserve states. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is an abstract quantization map with this property, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is compatible with a prequantum line bundle if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            preserves pure states. By the overcompleteness property, it’s similarly true that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \rangle
            intertwines the trace with integration, so it also preserves states — however, it certainly doesn’t preserve pure states!
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\rangle
            maps quantum pure states to classical mixed states, this introduces uncertainty.
          %p
            The 3–point function (which is really a degree 2 cochain on the pair
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            and determines a class in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+cohomology"} groupoid cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            of the prequantum line bundle and is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta([x],[y],[z])
                  \\,=\,
                  \\langle x|y\rangle\langle y|z\rangle\langle z|x\rangle\,.
          %p
            This is the same 3–point function that appears in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
          %p
            As for
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Item1"} item 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+line+bundle"} canonical line bundle
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mrow
                      %mi γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mi γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mtext Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℒ
                    %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega([x],[y])=\int_{\gamma(0)=[x]}^{\gamma(1)=[y]}\mathcal{D}\gamma\,P(\gamma)\in\text{Hom}(\mathcal{L}_{x},\mathcal{L}_{y})
          %p
            is the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x|\mapsto \langle x|y\rangle\langle y|\,.
          %p
            There are now two ways of describing
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Item2"} item 2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi\mapsto\Big([y]\mapsto\int_{P(\mathcal{H})}\Psi([x])|y\rangle\langle y|\omega^n\Big)\,.
          %p These two Hilbert spaces are the same as this is the orthogonal projection onto holomorphic sections.
          %p
            This tells most of the story for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathcal{H}).
          %h3#projective_submanifolds Projective Submanifolds
          %p
            There is another class of examples that is just as natural and almost just as simple as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathcal{H}).
            These are symplectic submanifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:M^{2n}\xhookrightarrow{} P(\mathcal{H})\subset B(\mathcal{H})
            which satisfy the overcompleteness condition, ie. up to a constant
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mi ℋ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi M
                  %mi q
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\mathcal{H}}=\int_M q\omega^n\,.
          %p
            These are more interesting because the quantum mechanics and classical mechanics are different, ie.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\langle \rangle
            is not a morphism of Lie algebras. However, there is a Lie subalgebra on which it is an injective morphism, and otherwise all other formulas discussed in the previous section remain valid. This Lie subalgebra can be identified with the set of operators which, under Schrodinger’s equation, preserve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M.
          %p
            One can think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathcal{H})
            as the pure states, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q(M)
            as the subset of pure states that are classical states.
          %h3#the_general_case The General Case
          %p
            In general then, to quantize as a symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            it is enough to embed it into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\mathcal{H})
            in such a way that the embedded submanifold has the overcompleteness property. It’s not clear to what extent this can be done exactly, however it can be done for Kahler manifolds which have a nice enough group of symmetries, eg.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*\mathbb{R}^n
            and the upper half plane. However, for all prequantizable compact manifolds (and many non–compact ones) this can be done approximately, ie.
          .num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,\omega)
              be a prequantizable compact symplectic manifold. Then there exists a sequence of embeddings
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ℋ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_k:M\xhookrightarrow{}P(\mathcal{H}_k)
            %p such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %msubsup
                      %mi q
                      %mi k
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi FS
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mi ω
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi m
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|q_k^*\omega_{FS}/k-\omega\|_{C^m}=\mathcal{O}(1/k)
            %p
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\in\mathbb{N},
              and such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_k(M)
              is overcomplete with respect to a measure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu_k
              for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\mu_{k}/k^n \to \omega^n\,.
          Here,
          %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
            %semantics
              %mrow
                %msub
                  %mi ω
                  %mi FS
              %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_{FS}
          is the Fubini–Study form. This theorem identifies the structure used to quantize and get star products on general compact symplectic manifolds,
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#Borthwicke–Uribe"} Borthwicke &amp; Uribe
          = succeed "." do
            %a{:href => "#Schlichenmaier2010"} Schlichenmaier
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#Dai–Liu–Ma"} Dai–Liu–Ma
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#Ma–Marinescu"} Ma–Marinescu
          %p
            Note that, there don’t seem to be any (convincing?) examples of quantizations of symplectic manifolds which can’t be strenghtened to a quantization of this form, so this perspective seems universal. Eg. Consider the quantization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*\mathbb{R}
            using the polarization with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi const
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=const.
            One can define coherent states using the lowering operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %mover
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{x}+i\hat{p}
            and then one immediately gets all of this additional structure.
          %h3#relationship_to_kostantsouriau_operators Relationship to Kostant-Souriau Operators
          %p Under the equivalence of categories from item 3 to item 2,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle x|\to \big([y]\mapsto \langle x|y\rangle\langle y|\big)
          %p
            gives a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℒ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I:\mathcal{H}\to \Gamma(M,\mathcal{L}).
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\in B(\mathcal{H})
            is such that the Hamiltonian vector field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{\langle H\rangle}
            is tangent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℋ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:M\xhookrightarrow{} P(\mathcal{H}),
            then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi H
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi I
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H=I^{-1}\circ (\nabla_{X_{\langle H\rangle}}+i\langle H\rangle)\circ I\,.
          %p
            Therefore,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\langle\rangle
            extends the Kostant-Souriau prequantization map. Note that, generically the Kostant-Souriau prequantization map doesn’t work at all, ie. it doesn’t quantize any non–constant functions! Eg. a generic genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\ge 3
            Riemann surface has no nontrivial automorphisms, and therefore on a generic genus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\ge 3
            surface the Kostant-Souriau prequantization map only quantizes constants. As a result, it only gives operators which are constant multiples of the identity.
          %h2#references References
          %p The observation is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Kibble79
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+W.+B.+Kibble"}> Tom W. B. Kibble
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Geometrization of quantum mechanics
              %strong 65
              (1979) 189–201 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01225149"}> doi:10.1007/BF01225149
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The idea was picked up in:
          %ul
            %li#Odzijewicz92
              %p
                Anatol Odzijewicz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Coherent state method in geometric quantization
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Coherent states and geometric quantization
                %strong 150
                (1992), no. 2, 385–413
                %a{:href => "http://ams.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/leavingmsn?url=http://projecteuclid.org/getRecord?id=euclid.cmp/1104251870"} doi
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1194023"} 94c:58077
            %li#Hughston96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lane+P.+Hughston"}> Lane P. Hughston
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometry of stochastic state vector reduction
                %strong 452
                1947 (1996) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1996.0048"}> doi:10.1098/rspa.1996.0048
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/52944"} jstor:52944
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (with speculations on the implication for
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+collapse"} quantum state collapse
            %li#AshtekarSchilling99
              %p
                Abhay Ashtekar, Troy A. Schilling,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometrical Formulation of Quantum Mechanics
                %em On Einstein’s Path
                Springer (1999) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/9706069"}> arXiv:gr-qc/9706069
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-1422-9_3"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-1422-9_3
          %p Further discussion:
          %ul
            %li#BrodyHughston01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dorje+C.+Brody"}> Dorje C. Brody
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lane+P.+Hughston"} Lane P. Hughston
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric Quantum Mechanics
                %strong 38
                (2001) 19-53 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9906086"}> arXiv:quant-ph/9906086
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0393-0440(00)00052-8"} doi:10.1016/S0393-0440(00)00052-8
            %li
              %p
                A Benvegnù, N Sansonetto, M Spera,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Remarks on geometric quantum mechanics
                %strong 51
                2 (2004) 229-243 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geomphys.2003.10.008"}> doi:10.1016/j.geomphys.2003.10.008
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                J. F. Cariñena, J. Clemente-Gallardo &amp; G. Marmo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometrization of quantum mechanics
                %strong 152
                (2007) 894–903 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11232-007-0075-3"}> doi:10.1007/s11232-007-0075-3
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Secret Blogging Seminar,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://sbseminar.wordpress.com/2009/11/16/quantum-mechanics-and-geometry/"} Quantum mechanics and geometry
                (2009)
            %li
              %p
                Hoshang Heydari,
                %em Geometric formulation of quantum mechanics
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.00238"}> arXiv:1503.00238
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#klauder
              %p J.R. Klauder. Quantization is Geometry, After All. Annals Phys. 188 (1988) 120-141. DOI: 10.1016/0003-4916(88)90092-9
          %p
            Discussion of dynamics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+states"} mixed states
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrices"}> density matrices
            ), now via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+geometry"} Poisson geometry
          %ul
            %li
              Pritish Sinha, Ankit Yadav,
              %em Poisson Geometric Formulation of Quantum Mechanics
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05615"}> arXiv:2312.05615
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On theorems related to quantizations which induce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+products"} star products
            on general symplectic manifolds:
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Borthwicke–Uribe"}
              %p David Borthwick, Alejandro Uribe. Almost complex structures and geometric quantization (arXiv:dg-ga/9608006)
            %li{:id => "Dai–Liu–Ma"}
              %p Xianzhe Dai, Kefeng Liu, Xiaonan Ma. On the asymptotic expansion of Bergman kernel. (arXiv:math/0404494v2)
            %li{:id => "Ma–Marinescu"}
              %p Xiaonan Ma, George Marinescu. Generalized Bergman kernels on symplectic manifolds. (arXiv:math/0411559v3)
            %li#Schlichenmaier2010
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Schlichenmaier"}> Martin Schlichenmaier
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Berezin-Toeplitz quantization for compact Kaehler manifolds – A Review of Results
                %strong 2010
                927280 (2010) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.2523"}> arXiv:1003.2523
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1155/2010/927280"} doi:10.1155/2010/927280
        .revisedby
